{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-107281",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+4)}{(x+4)(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne -4,1,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=1.$ So statement I is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x),$ and  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $1$ | $1.001$ | $1.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-99$ | $-999$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 1$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 1$ from the right. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement II is false and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196482/assets/3de7d0a26f6f171fc83bd885eed9ff9f50bddfc76b2f58e9477241fb874c9b7d.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
